00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:40,600
and that is going to take up

214
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:41,760
the remainder of the podcast.

215
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:44,200
So let's get started, shall we?

216
00:09:44,320 --> 00:09:48,720
Master'ship in seven minutes or less.

217
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:54,840
It's a simple thought. but I want you to talk to your Scouts

218
00:09:59,440 --> 00:10:03,080
and not talk down to them, not give them orders,

219
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:05,600
but I want you to sit and talk to them,

220
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:08,120
And I know that a lot of you already do this.

221
00:10:08,120 --> 00:10:11,400
but a lot of times, some of my first advice

222
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:12,880
on questions I receive

223
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:16,480
is: well, have you asked the Scouts how they feel about that?

224
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:19,000
We'll find that they have lots of answers if we ask

225
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,360
and if we listen carefully. and to get good at listening,

226
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:25,560
I think the first step is looking at our own attitudes

227
00:10:25,560 --> 00:10:28,080
and our understanding of the role that we play.

228
00:10:28,080 --> 00:10:29,960
Listening to young people requires

229
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:31,840
that we know something in their lives

230
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:34,720
and the challenges and changes that they're working through,

231
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:36,600
And we have to cultivate that understanding.

232
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:39,000
We have to be intentionally compassionate,

233
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,320
and compassion comes from understanding.

234
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:43,600
Now, a lot of times,

235
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:47,280
Scouts who don't have good relationship skills-

236
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:50,040
and I would say probably especially men,

237
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:52,400
because I are one right-

238
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:55,360
We use aggression and rigid attitudes

239
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:57,280
to compensate for a whole range of things,

240
00:10:57,280 --> 00:11:00,320
And usually it's our own inadequacies and fears.

241
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:04,800
An aggressive or kind of senselessly rigid attitude

242
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:06,800
will give you short-term control.

243
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:08,440
You'll be able to control your Scouts

244
00:11:08,440 --> 00:11:11,240
and you'll be able to control everything around you right,

245
00:11:11,280 --> 00:11:12,600
But that's it.

246
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:14,280
You're not gonna get much else.

247
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:16,600
I'm a big, imposing man.

248
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:17,720
I'm six foot one.

249
00:11:17,720 --> 00:11:20,520
I have a very healthy avert du pois.

250
00:11:20,520 --> 00:11:23,080
I have a uniform and a big hat.

251
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:24,720
I've got a big beard.

252
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:26,480
I've got a deep voice.

253
00:11:26,480 --> 00:11:29,000
I can stand in the middle of a room of Scouts

254
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,760
and I can use my physical stature and my loud voice

255
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:34,480
to get attention and get them to do what I want them to do,

256
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:37,360
But that gives no lasting effect.

257
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:40,360
I haven't achieved anything but compliance.

258
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:43,240
Our mission is so much bigger than that, isn't it?

259
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:45,360
We're on a mission to change lives

260
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:48,840
and we don't get much change or growth

261
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:52,920
through compliance to our aggressive or rigid attitudes.

262
00:11:52,920 --> 00:11:57,160
And you can apply an aggressive or a rigid attitude

263
00:11:57,160 --> 00:12:00,680
to have a very smooth running, high-attieving troop

264
00:12:00,680 --> 00:12:02,760
without any real personal growth or development

265
00:12:02,760 --> 00:12:04,760
on the part of individual Scouts.

266
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:07,640
And that's why I think it's very, very important

267
00:12:07,640 --> 00:12:09,800
that you talk to your Scouts

268
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:12,920
and you learn to listen with a little humility,

269
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:16,480
And we just may begin to see what they need

270
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:19,160
and how we can help them build on their understanding

271
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:21,120
of the Scout Oath and Law.

272
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:24,000
Given the opportunity to offer their ideas and solutions

273
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,560
to problems or conundrums that we're running across

274
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:32,000
Scouts begin to call on those developing inner strengths.

275
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,400
that is our main aim to develop.

276
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:37,960
As we move along, we begin to see the Scouting Spirit

277
00:12:37,960 --> 00:12:42,560
in an individual Scout's heart is really the source. okay,

278
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:45,040
And what I mean by that is we may think that our program

279
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:47,120
or our prowess or our traditions

280
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:50,360
or our practices are the source. they're not.

281
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:54,000
It's a big mistake, and it's one that is as big

282
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:55,560
as it is common.

283
00:12:55,560 --> 00:12:58,200
We can't impose the Spirit of Scouting

284
00:12:58,200 --> 00:13:01,080
and all the good that flows from that on a Scout.

285
00:13:01,080 --> 00:13:03,680
It's already there, waiting for our ears,

286
00:13:03,680 --> 00:13:05,400
waiting for us to listen

287
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:08,959
and waiting for us to help our Scouts discover.

288
00:13:08,959 --> 00:13:11,600
Alls we have to do is talk to them

289
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:14,959
and learn to listen to what they have to say.

290
00:13:14,959 --> 00:13:17,000
If you'll take the time to do that- and, like I said,

291
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,560
do that with compassion and humility.

292
00:13:19,560 --> 00:13:21,800
listen to what your Scouts have to say-

293
00:13:21,800 --> 00:13:23,520
the list of problems and challenges

294
00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:25,360
begins to grow a lot smaller.

295
00:13:55,520 --> 00:13:56,360
My name

296
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:00,520
Email, that is folks,

297
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:03,640
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

298
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:13,680
I heard from Scoutmaster Jim Hu,

299
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:17,480
who is with Troop 309 in Morton, Illinois,

300
00:14:17,480 --> 00:14:18,840
And he wrote in to ask this:

301
00:14:18,840 --> 00:14:20,840
how do you handle a Scout who has earned

302
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:22,240
the first aid Marriott badge

303
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:24,200
at one of those Marriott badge fairs

304
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:25,920
and now wants you to sign off

305
00:14:25,920 --> 00:14:27,360
on the Tenderfoot second class

306
00:14:27,360 --> 00:14:30,000
and first class first aid requirements.

307
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,040
Well, Jim, fairly simple answer.

308
00:14:32,040 --> 00:14:33,360
I'd sign off the requirements

309
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:35,520
the same way I would for any other Scout.

310
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:39,760
And if a Scout has first aid Marriott badge,

311
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:42,240
demonstrating those first aid requirements

312
00:14:42,240 --> 00:14:43,400
would be really simple

313
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:45,520
and probably wouldn't take all that long.

314
00:14:45,520 --> 00:14:47,360
Now if they complained to me,

315
00:14:47,360 --> 00:14:48,840
or their parent complained to me,

316
00:14:48,840 --> 00:14:50,520
that they had already done that,

317
00:14:50,520 --> 00:14:53,280
I would show them this from the guide to advancement.

318
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:55,160
Here we are in the guide to advancement folks,

319
00:14:55,160 --> 00:14:56,800
and you know about the numbers, right

320
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:58,520
Pencil's ready. here we go

321
00:14:58,520 --> 00:15:03,280
In section 4.2.3.6,

322
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:05,640
titled Fulfilling More than One Wireman

323
00:15:05,640 --> 00:15:07,280
with a Single Activity,

324
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:08,360
And so let me read this to you.

325
00:15:08,360 --> 00:15:09,600
From time to time

326
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:12,120
it may be appropriate for a Scout to apply

327
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:14,439
what he has done to meet one requirement

328
00:15:14,439 --> 00:15:16,160
toward the completion of another.

329
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:18,240
in deciding whether to allow this,

330
00:15:18,240 --> 00:15:20,040
unit leaders or Marriott badge counselors

331
00:15:20,040 --> 00:15:21,800
should consider the following:

332
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:23,920
When, for all practical purposes,

333
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:26,360
two requirements match up exactly

334
00:15:26,360 --> 00:15:28,520
and have the same basic intent-

335
00:15:28,520 --> 00:15:30,880
for example, camping nights for the second class

336
00:15:30,880 --> 00:15:34,160
and first class ranks and for the camping Marriott badge-

337
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:36,680
it is appropriate and permissible,

338
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:39,200
unless it is stated otherwise in the requirements

339
00:15:39,200 --> 00:15:41,560
to use those matching activities

340
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:44,040
for both ranks and the Marriott badge,

341
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:46,920
Where matching requirements are oriented towards safety,

342
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:49,800
such as those related to first aid or CPR,

343
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:51,720
the person signing off the requirements

344
00:15:51,720 --> 00:15:54,520
should be satisfied that the Scout remembers

345
00:15:54,520 --> 00:15:57,240
what he learned from the previous experience,

346
00:15:57,240 --> 00:15:58,960
And I think that says it all, doesn't it?

347
00:15:58,960 --> 00:16:01,480
So not only in the instance that you stated, Jim,

348
00:16:01,480 --> 00:16:03,480
there are requirements that can be fulfilled

349
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:05,040
within a single activity.

350
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:06,800
I think that the guide to advancement

351
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:08,240
is very clear on this,

352
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:10,280
especially where matching requirements

353
00:16:10,280 --> 00:16:12,320
are oriented towards safety.

354
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:14,560
We wanna make sure that the Scout remembers

355
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:17,160
what he had learned from the previous experience.

356
00:16:17,160 --> 00:16:18,200
Heard from Bill Chapman.

357
00:16:18,200 --> 00:16:20,720
he says: I'm a relatively new Scout master

358
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:24,320
and our troop had a history of focusing on advancement,

359
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:26,320
with most troop meetings in a classroom

360
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:28,560
and the instruction by the Scout master

361
00:16:28,560 --> 00:16:29,920
or other adult instructor.

362
00:16:29,920 --> 00:16:31,920
We combined three small troops

363
00:16:31,920 --> 00:16:33,360
and we're trying to use the patrol method

364
00:16:33,360 --> 00:16:35,160
to have a scout led troop.

365
00:16:35,160 --> 00:16:36,560
In the beginning I spent a lot of time

366
00:16:36,560 --> 00:16:38,200
trying to train our senior patrol leader

367
00:16:38,200 --> 00:16:40,080
to run troop meetings and camp outs

368
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:42,960
and to get our patrol leaders council up to speed.

369
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:44,840
But I did not grow up in Scouting

370
00:16:44,840 --> 00:16:46,280
and I don't have enough Scouting skills

371
00:16:46,280 --> 00:16:48,360
and experience to really train them. well,

372
00:16:48,360 --> 00:16:50,840
I have an assistant Scout master and no Scouting,

373
00:16:50,840 --> 00:16:54,680
but I worry about us directing the program too much.

374
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:56,600
How do we train our senior patrol leader

375
00:16:56,600 --> 00:16:58,760
to run the patrol leaders council

376
00:16:58,760 --> 00:17:00,800
and provide a good quality program

377
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:03,200
without taking over and running it ourselves?

378
00:17:03,200 --> 00:17:05,319
Well, Bill, that is a great question.

379
00:17:05,319 --> 00:17:07,200
How do you train the senior patrol leader

380
00:17:07,200 --> 00:17:08,880
to run the patrol leaders council

381
00:17:08,880 --> 00:17:11,400
and provide a good quality program

382
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:14,280
without taking it over and running it ourselves?

383
00:17:14,280 --> 00:17:16,200
So let's pay attention to two key things

384
00:17:16,200 --> 00:17:18,440
that you said in asking that question.

385
00:17:18,440 --> 00:17:20,240
I'm gonna pick out two terms:

386
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:23,240
One is training and the other is good quality program.

387
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:24,480
So let's begin with that first.

388
00:17:24,480 --> 00:17:26,480
What is a good quality program?

389
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:29,000
And you're gonna determine that very subjectively, right,

390
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,920
Every parent somewhere has a little drawing

391
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:33,680
or a little lopsided clay statue

392
00:17:33,680 --> 00:17:36,560
or something that their child created and gave them,

393
00:17:36,560 --> 00:17:39,000
And I've used this as an illustration before.

394
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:40,960
but you'll remember, right,

395
00:17:40,960 --> 00:17:42,880
That little statue or that little drawing

396
00:17:42,880 --> 00:17:44,920
is not going to be in a national museum

397
00:17:44,920 --> 00:17:46,680
or the critics aren't gonna be writing about it,

398
00:17:46,680 --> 00:17:48,120
but to you it's priceless

399
00:17:48,120 --> 00:17:50,320
And we need to look at what our scouts are doing

400
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:52,400
and the quality of our program

401
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:54,600
from precisely that perspective.

402
00:17:54,600 --> 00:17:57,200
When you start to see just how much scouting

403
00:17:57,200 --> 00:17:59,840
is actually going on in the midst of all the chaos

404
00:17:59,840 --> 00:18:01,960
and things that look kind of disjointed,

405
00:18:01,960 --> 00:18:04,840
well, you begin to get the idea-

406
00:18:04,840 --> 00:18:06,080
And we talk about this a lot-

407
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:07,640
because scouting is unique

408
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:10,000
and it requires a unique perspective

409
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,520
on the part of the adults involved.

410
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:13,680
I'll bet that there's a lot more quality

411
00:18:13,680 --> 00:18:16,600
in what your scouts are doing than your first thought.

412
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:19,040
Now, the other term that I wanted to take a little time

413
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:20,720
to talk about was training,

414
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:23,880
And I would probably prefer, for the most part,

415
00:18:23,880 --> 00:18:26,760
that scouts forgot what they know

416
00:18:26,760 --> 00:18:28,480
about training youth leaders,

417
00:18:28,480 --> 00:18:31,360
because we have a tradition of session

418
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:33,040
and event-based training

419
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:35,560
that I don't think works very well,

420
00:18:35,560 --> 00:18:37,640
Not because I think that the sessions

421
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:40,480
and the event-based training are totally ineffective

422
00:18:40,480 --> 00:18:42,560
or they don't have any good content to them,

423
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:43,879
but because of the attitude

424
00:18:43,879 --> 00:18:46,000
with which most scouts regard them.

425
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:47,200
Hey, you've been to training,

426
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:50,360
you know what to do. now I'm all done. here we go.

427
00:18:50,360 --> 00:18:52,760
But the difficulty is is there's no way

428
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:54,280
you can train a person to be a leader.

429
00:18:54,280 --> 00:18:57,520
The only way you become a leader is by leading right,

430
00:18:57,520 --> 00:18:59,440
Not by being trained to be a leader.

431
00:18:59,440 --> 00:19:01,040
you have to actually start leading,

432
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:03,320
and that's what you're gonna do with your scouts.

433
00:19:03,320 --> 00:19:06,000
Be mindful that every contact you have

434
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:07,600
with your senior patrol leader

435
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:09,440
and your patrol leaders council

436
00:19:09,440 --> 00:19:13,080
is an opportunity for developing youth leadership.

437
00:19:13,080 --> 00:19:14,640
Go slowly and incrementally

438
00:19:14,640 --> 00:19:17,020
and ask lots and lots of questions

439
00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:19,040
designed to help them discover things

440
00:19:19,040 --> 00:19:21,200
and build on it a little at a time.

441
00:19:21,200 --> 00:19:23,720
You say you don't have a whole lot of experience

442
00:19:23,720 --> 00:19:25,720
with scouting. well, you know what

443
00:19:25,720 --> 00:19:28,160
You're going to learn right along with them

444
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:30,520
and if you're humble enough to say that to them,

445
00:19:30,520 --> 00:19:32,320
they're gonna work right along with you

446
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:34,760
and you're gonna develop your skills as a scout master

447
00:19:34,760 --> 00:19:37,360
as they develop their skills as youth leaders.

448
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:38,600
If you're doing a restart,

449
00:19:38,600 --> 00:19:40,120
if you're kind of starting from scratch,

450
00:19:40,120 --> 00:19:42,000
this is gonna work really well.

451
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,680
You may first have to model some of the things

452
00:19:44,680 --> 00:19:46,000
for the scouts.

453
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,120
You may need to show the senior patrol leader

454
00:19:48,120 --> 00:19:50,200
how to be a senior patrol leader.

455
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:53,180
You may need to show patrol leaders how to be patrol leaders,

456
00:19:53,180 --> 00:19:56,000
but then you can hand it off to them as they catch on.

457
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,280
The biggest mistake we make, I think, sometimes

458
00:19:58,280 --> 00:19:59,520
in working with youth leaders

459
00:19:59,520 --> 00:20:01,080
is we think we can sit down

460
00:20:01,080 --> 00:20:03,160
and in one sitting, explain everything to them

461
00:20:03,160 --> 00:20:05,160
and they got it and we're done from there.

462
00:20:05,160 --> 00:20:06,980
but it never really works that way.

463
00:20:06,980 --> 00:20:09,260
Don't overload them, don't over instruct.

464
00:20:09,260 --> 00:20:11,860
Give them one or two things to work on at a time.

465
00:20:11,860 --> 00:20:13,020
let them try them out,

466
00:20:13,020 --> 00:20:15,700
maybe even show them how it works by doing it yourself

467
00:20:15,700 --> 00:20:17,100
and then let them at it.

468
00:20:17,100 --> 00:20:20,380
As time goes by they'll begin to pick up on things

469
00:20:20,380 --> 00:20:22,580
and they'll begin to model the behavior

470
00:20:22,580 --> 00:20:24,820
and skills of leadership for the new scouts

471
00:20:24,820 --> 00:20:26,020
that joined the troop.

472
00:20:26,020 --> 00:20:29,420
Then you have this kind of self perpetuating model right.

473
00:20:29,420 --> 00:20:31,900
But our role in the process is mentors

474
00:20:31,900 --> 00:20:35,460
and questioners. continually developing youth leadership

475
00:20:35,460 --> 00:20:36,860
never stops.

476
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:37,980
I got this question.

477
00:20:37,980 --> 00:20:40,940
I don't concur with the way my scout master runs

478
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:42,100
leadership elections.

479
00:20:42,100 --> 00:20:43,740
He says anyone should be eligible

480
00:20:43,740 --> 00:20:45,700
to be elected senior patrol leader.

481
00:20:45,700 --> 00:20:47,340
I say we should limit the eligibility

482
00:20:47,340 --> 00:20:50,380
for senior patrol leader and other important positions

483
00:20:50,380 --> 00:20:52,540
for the scouts who are on their way to star

484
00:20:52,540 --> 00:20:55,980
and life ranks and at least 15 years old.

485
00:20:55,980 --> 00:20:58,020
Well, you know I understand your concern,

486
00:20:58,020 --> 00:21:00,580
but I'm gonna say I agree with your scout master.

487
00:21:00,580 --> 00:21:03,540
Any scout ought to be able to put themselves forward

488
00:21:03,540 --> 00:21:05,660
to be elected the senior patrol leader.

489
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:08,460
If we reserve that only for scouts who needed a position

490
00:21:08,460 --> 00:21:10,500
of responsibility for advancement,

491
00:21:10,500 --> 00:21:12,460
or we thought we're qualified

492
00:21:12,460 --> 00:21:16,500
or we wanted only older scouts running for that position,

493
00:21:16,500 --> 00:21:18,140
we're denying the chance to a younger,

494
00:21:18,140 --> 00:21:20,540
perhaps more talented and mature scout,

495
00:21:20,540 --> 00:21:22,780
or a scout is otherwise a good leader

496
00:21:22,780 --> 00:21:25,540
but really isn't interested in advancing in rank.

497
00:21:25,540 --> 00:21:28,340
David wrote to me and said I'm looking for some guidance

498
00:21:28,340 --> 00:21:29,980
on high adventure patrols.

499
00:21:29,980 --> 00:21:32,820
Eventually our scouts want to go on high adventure tracks

500
00:21:32,820 --> 00:21:35,260
and we've been breaking up existing patrols

501
00:21:35,260 --> 00:21:36,900
and creating high adventure patrols

502
00:21:36,900 --> 00:21:39,060
that train and prepare for the track.

503
00:21:39,060 --> 00:21:42,500
This has the side effect of abandoning boys in the old patrol

504
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:44,740
that aren't interested in the high adventure track

505
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:46,340
and I believe it hurts the patrol method.

506
00:21:46,340 --> 00:21:49,620
I'm looking for advice on how others have dealt with this.

507
00:21:49,620 --> 00:21:51,980
David, I really don't see much practical use

508
00:21:51,980 --> 00:21:54,860
in resetting patrols around a high adventure trip.

509
00:21:54,860 --> 00:21:58,260
In my experience, when we've had a high adventure trip

510
00:21:58,260 --> 00:22:01,180
scheduled, which we do every single summer,

511
00:22:01,180 --> 00:22:02,940
the guys who are gonna form that crew

512
00:22:02,940 --> 00:22:05,700
aren't preparing for and training during troop meetings

513
00:22:05,700 --> 00:22:07,900
or camping trips for the high adventure trip.

514
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:11,060
They're following a separate schedule of things like that.

515
00:22:11,060 --> 00:22:14,060
For the most part, these scouts are mostly

516
00:22:14,060 --> 00:22:16,140
our troops' youth leadership, so it wouldn't work

517
00:22:16,140 --> 00:22:19,700
to have them distracted from doing that during troop time.

518
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:22,580
So I think that's a reasonably simple solution.

519
00:22:22,580 --> 00:22:24,820
You don't need to reset the troops patrols.

520
00:22:24,820 --> 00:22:26,100
You need a separate schedule

521
00:22:26,100 --> 00:22:28,420
for the guys who are going on high adventure.

522
00:22:28,420 --> 00:22:30,780
Heard from Craig Dixon, who is the scout master

523
00:22:30,780 --> 00:22:34,020
troop 682 in Poe, California.

524
00:22:34,020 --> 00:22:38,620
One of my scouts ran for senior patrol leader two times

525
00:22:38,620 --> 00:22:40,500
and he wasn't elected either time.

526
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:42,420
I'm quite sure he's disappointed.

527
00:22:42,420 --> 00:22:43,660
What would you say to him

528
00:22:43,660 --> 00:22:45,899
And do you have any suggestions for how I

529
00:22:45,899 --> 00:22:48,340
or the new senior patrol leader can keep him involved

530
00:22:48,340 --> 00:22:50,260
